<p>Oil prices jumped at the start of the week as traders priced in the risk that escalating fighting involving Iran could disrupt exports from the Middle East, a region central to global energy supply. The move followed U.S. and Israeli strikes on Iran and Iran’s retaliatory attacks, which multiple reports said added to disruptions and raised fresh concern about shipping through the Gulf. Several outlets singled out the Strait of Hormuz as the main pressure point. Trade sources told Dawn that tanker owners, oil majors, and trading houses suspended shipments of crude, fuel, and liquefied natural gas through the strait after Tehran warned ships against transiting the waterway, and analysts noted that more than a fifth of global oil flows through it. The risk of a shipping choke point—along with uncertainty about how long any disruption could last—fed forecasts that prices could climb toward $100 a barrel.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/qWz0GyXXNR_0WvqAOUFFIWNmvT-Mf0koucbtnJKNrGxKtItp1IJFqBejlVQ--VClBJjfINKQvnqe_btRak6xaOi2mwoVF8kryUsMkYpkS4jW' alt='Oil tankers pass through the Strait of Hormuz, December 21, 2018.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Pre-weekend setup: Brent had already climbed this year and reached about $73 a barrel on Friday (its highest since July) before the weekend’s strikes. With futures markets closed, the first big repricing showed up in over-the-counter trading.</li><li>Shipping incidents: Strikes in the region hit two vessels traveling through the Strait of Hormuz, reporting said, limiting some countries’ ability to export oil to global markets.</li><li>Wider disruption concern: Early trading reflected fears that supply from Iran and elsewhere in the Middle East could slow sharply, as retaliatory strikes targeted Israel and U.S. military installations around the Gulf.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>ICIS (energy analytics): ICIS said the biggest driver is whether the Strait of Hormuz effectively closes, calling it the factor that matters most for prices beyond the strikes themselves. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1977102/oil-jumps-10pc-on-iran-conflict-and-could-spike-to-100-a-barrel-analysts-say'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>RBC Capital Markets (analyst view): RBC’s Helima Croft said Middle East leaders told Washington that a war on Iran could push oil prices above $100 a barrel. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1977102/oil-jumps-10pc-on-iran-conflict-and-could-spike-to-100-a-barrel-analysts-say'>Dawn</a>)</li><li>DW (market analysis): DW said Iran’s role as a producer matters, but its position near key shipping routes is what keeps traders on edge about sudden price spikes. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/will-iran-conflict-send-oil-prices-above-100-a-barrel/a-76176495'>DW</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/dac7a77d-e0f4-4f52-a3d4-55b145e67347'>Oil prices soar as escalating conflict threatens supplies</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/oil-prices-iran-attacks-strait-of-hormuz/'>Oil prices rise sharply in market trading after attacks disrupt supply</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/03/01/nx-s1-5731584/oil-prices-iran-us-israel-attacks-war'>Oil prices rise sharply in market trading after attacks in Middle East disrupt supply</a> - npr.org</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/business/markets/oil-prices-rise-sharply-after-attacks-in-middle-east-disrupt-global-energy-supply/article70693725.ece'>Oil prices rise sharply after attacks in West Asia disrupt global energy supply</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1977102/oil-jumps-10pc-on-iran-conflict-and-could-spike-to-100-a-barrel-analysts-say'>Oil jumps 10pc on Iran conflict and could spike to $100 a barrel, analysts say</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/will-iran-conflict-send-oil-prices-above-100-a-barrel/a-76176495'>Will Iran conflict send oil prices above $100 a barrel?</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='